    {
      "text": "The system shall detect loss or ambiguity of lane markings (e.g., faded, missing, or obscured) and, if lane confidence falls below a defined threshold, safely transition to driver‑only control while providing a clear warning."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall maintain a minimum safe following distance that adapts to vehicle speed, road conditions, and traffic dynamics, and shall automatically apply emergency braking if the distance drops below the safety threshold."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall require continuous driver engagement verification that combines steering‑torque detection with an in‑cab driver‑monitoring camera to ensure the driver’s eyes are on the road, and shall issue escalating alerts and disengage if engagement is lost."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall limit the maximum autonomous speed based on road context (e.g., lane type, curvature, weather, visibility) and must not exceed the posted speed limit plus a defined safety margin."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall provide an unambiguous, fail‑safe handover procedure that includes a timed audible tone, visual indicator, and haptic feedback to the steering wheel, giving the driver at least 2 seconds to resume full control before Autopilot disengages."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall detect and classify stationary or slow‑moving road hazards (e.g., construction zones, barriers, crash attenuators) using sensor fusion, and shall either avoid them or initiate a controlled stop with driver notification."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall log all Autopilot activation, disengagement, sensor status changes, and driver‑alert events with timestamps to non‑volatile storage for post‑incident analysis."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall undergo periodic self‑diagnosis of software integrity and cryptographic verification of over‑the‑air updates, refusing to apply any update that fails integrity checks."
    },
    {
      "text": "The system shall support redundancy in critical control pathways (e.g., dual CAN buses for brake and steering commands) so that a single‑point failure does not prevent safe vehicle stop or driver takeover."
    }
  ]
